    relatedSlugs: buyLeadsRel,
    content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A customer leaves a two-star review over a miscommunication, the business resolves it, and the owner wonders whether it's fair game to ask that customer to update the review, a request that can either strengthen a relationship or feel manipulative depending entirely on how it's handled." },
      { type: "heading", text: "The Difference Between Asking and Pressuring" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A genuine, low-pressure request made after a real resolution is different from repeatedly nudging a customer or making the edit feel like a condition of further service, and the line between the two is usually obvious to the customer even when it isn't obvious to the business making the ask." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A useful test is whether the request would still feel appropriate if the customer said no and nothing changed about their relationship with the business afterward, since that's the standard a genuinely low-pressure ask should be able to meet." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Resolution Has to Come Before the Ask, Not Alongside It" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Fixing the underlying problem first, then following up separately once the customer confirms they're satisfied, keeps the request feeling like a natural next step rather than something offered in exchange for a better rating, which is the distinction that matters most." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Combining the fix and the ask into the same conversation risks making the customer feel like their satisfaction was never really the point, only the review was." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Never Offer Anything of Value for the Edit" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Offering a discount, refund, or free service specifically in exchange for editing a review crosses into incentivized review territory, which violates platform policy regardless of whether the original review was fair, and risks the account far more than the original review ever did." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The resolution itself, fixing what went wrong, should be the only thing offered, and it should be offered regardless of whether the customer ever updates their review, otherwise it isn't really a resolution at all." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Let the Customer Decide Without Follow-Up Pressure" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A single respectful message noting the issue has been resolved and that an updated review would be appreciated, if they feel it's warranted, respects the customer's choice, while repeated reminders after no response start to feel like exactly the pressure that should be avoided." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Sometimes the Original Review Should Just Stand" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Not every resolved complaint needs an edited review to go with it, and a business that responds well publicly to a legitimate old complaint, without ever asking for a revision, often looks just as credible to future readers as one with a perfectly clean record." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "In some cases, pushing for an edit can even look worse than leaving the original review in place, since a suspiciously sudden jump from one star to five raises more questions than it answers for anyone reading closely." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Document the Resolution Either Way" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Even if a customer declines to edit their review, keeping a record of how the issue was resolved gives the team something concrete to reference in a public response, which does much of the same reputational work an edited review would have done." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This record is also useful internally, since a pattern of similar complaints across different customers points to a process problem worth fixing well beyond any single review's outcome." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Training the team to log these resolutions consistently, rather than leaving them to memory, turns each one into a small but permanent asset the business can draw on the next time a similar situation comes up." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A business with strong day-to-day service rarely needs to lean on review edits at all, and pairing that service quality with [exclusive leads](/buy-leads) builds a review profile that speaks for itself." },
    ],
